    "slot_number": "slot-1-2-2",
    "slot_key": "Acceptable conversion completeness threshold",
    "slot_value": "A batch is successful only when the required conversion and verification steps are complete for everything in scope, not just most of it; small exceptions are acceptable only if they are understood and explicitly accepted by the workflow.",
    "operation": "update"
  },
  {
    "slot_number": "slot-1-2-1",
    "slot_key": "Definition of successful conversion and validation",
    "slot_value": "Content and metadata are verified after conversion, with checks on what changed after conversion; archivists also need to be able to inspect and browse records when something looks odd. A successful transition also means staff can keep doing the same core jobs without jumping between ad hoc scripts and manual checks, and the results are trustworthy enough to use in production workflows. Results that are close enough to look valid but not trustworthy enough for production use should be clearly flagged; hard validation failures should block downstream use until someone reviews them; borderline results or those with warnings should be routed to manual review so staff can decide whether they are acceptable. Automatic fix-up should be limited to small, well-understood issues like minor formatting irregularities that don’t change the meaning of the record, or missing bits that can be safely normalized. Corruption, truncated content, broken headers, or ambiguous metadata should go to staff triage. Conversion can proceed with non-fatal, clearly documented validation errors only when the tool can preserve enough context for later review; if output may be misleading or not trustworthy, the process should stop or require explicit confirmation before continuing. Staff use WARC and ARC record data for quality control and preservation checking to confirm the archive is complete, readable, and suitable for long-term access or migration, and also to diagnose problems, explain anomalies in reports, and verify that conversion didn’t change anything unexpectedly. Records should be complete and internally consistent, with headers, payload, and metadata agreeing; redirects, embedded resources, duplicate captures, and slightly unusual formatting can often be handled automatically, but truncated, corrupt, or contradictory records that affect trust should trigger human review. For reporting and verification, tools should capture and display record identifier, capture date and time, source file or batch, record type, target URI or URL, basic size or checksum information if available, conversion status, warnings, and the relationship between source record and output record so staff can trace lineage. For special cases, redirect location, duplicate match information, and an incomplete-record flag should be visible when relevant, especially for incomplete or duplicated records, though there is not yet a hard rule that any one of those fields is universally mandatory in every case. When output falls short, the batch should be marked as partially successful or failed depending on the size of the gap, and detailed error information should remain attached; it should not be promoted to a clean success unless the missing pieces are clearly accounted for and consciously accepted.",
    "operation": "refine"
  }
]

[
